CM_ "Yamaha CP3 (Tracer 9 2021-2024 generation) diagnostic-connector CAN bus, 500 kbit/s, 11-bit IDs. Generated from data/signals.yaml of github.com/bratukh/yamaha-cp3-can (CC BY 4.0). Read from one motorcycle, a 2022 Tracer 9 (RN70, EU). Each signal comment starts with [status/confidence]. Read-only use.";
CM_ BO_ 517 "Front and rear wheel speed as two 16-bit words. The difference between them shows wheel slip under traction control and ABS.";
CM_ SG_ 517 WHEEL_SPEED_FRONT "[confirmed/high] Front wheel speed, raw / 101.0 = true km/h. The dash reads about 9 % higher than this, as most speedometers do.";
CM_ SG_ 517 WHEEL_SPEED_REAR "[confirmed/medium] Rear wheel speed, same scale as the front. At steady speed it reads within 0.2 % of the front below 60 km/h and up to 1 % higher above 100 km/h.";
CM_ SG_ 517 ACCEL_STATE_BITS "[hypothesis/low] Four flag bits in the low nibble of B5 (B4 is always 0). Bit 3 is set in 50-73 % of frames while accelerating, 4-6 % while decelerating, never with the rear brake pedal down, and about 90 % of the time at standstill. Bit 1 goes with hard acceleration, bit 2 with moderate acceleration. Looks like a drive or acceleration state. An earlier reading of bit 3 as 'ABS armed' does not fit.";
CM_ BO_ 521 "Engine speed, gear position, riding mode and traction-control level in one frame.";
CM_ SG_ 521 ENGINE_RPM "[confirmed/high] Engine speed, raw x 25/64.";
CM_ SG_ 521 GEAR_ACTUAL "[confirmed/high] Gear reported by the gearbox sensor. Never reads 7. With the engine stopped it reads 0 whatever gear is engaged, so use `gear_requested` for a display.";
CM_ SG_ 521 TCS_LEVEL "[confirmed/high] Traction-control setting. The same value is repeated in 0x226 B3 and in 0x215. Value 0 is probably TCS OFF, which this bike offers, but that is not confirmed: it appeared on one ride only, and nobody noted TCS OFF being selected on it. No capture has a deliberate switch to OFF yet.";
CM_ SG_ 521 START_FLAG "[hypothesis/medium] Set while the engine is cranking: in 13 of 13 engine starts it comes on with the first cranking frame and clears 0.7-0.9 s later when the engine idles. On some rides it is also set for long stretches with the ignition on and the engine off, so the exact meaning is open. It is not a shift flag: clear at all 535 gear changes checked.";
CM_ SG_ 521 GEAR_REQUESTED "[confirmed/high] Gear position as the shift mechanism sees it. Reads 7 for a moment during gear changes between 2nd and 6th while moving; changes between 1st and 2nd go straight from one number to the other. Keeps showing the engaged gear with the engine stopped, which is what the dash shows. Mask with 0x07: bit 3 of the byte is a separate flag.";
CM_ SG_ 521 RIDE_MODE "[confirmed/medium] Selected riding mode. The same two bits appear in 0x215 B7 and 0x226 B0.";
CM_ SG_ 521 SHIFT_WINDOW_BITS "[hypothesis/medium] B5 takes the values 0x00, 0x04 and 0x08. 0x08 goes with an open throttle and 0x04 with a closed throttle and the clutch engaged, but 0x08 never occurs in 6th gear and 0x04 never occurs in 1st. That fits 'upshift possible' (bit 3) and 'downshift possible' (bit 2), the two quickshifter windows, better than a plain drive/overrun state. Not checked against the quickshifter arrows on the dash.";
CM_ BO_ 533 "Throttle grip position and throttle valve opening as two separate 16-bit words, plus copies of the traction-control level and the riding mode.";
CM_ SG_ 533 THROTTLE_VALVE "[hypothesis/medium] Throttle valve opening: about 14 at idle, 697-698 at the stop. It follows the grip most of the time and departs from it where the ECU decides: it stays open with the grip closed for minutes at steady speed (cruise control), opens less than the grip asks at low rpm, and drops during a quickshift while the grip stays put. No independent reference, hence hypothesis.";
CM_ SG_ 533 THROTTLE_GRIP "[confirmed/medium] Throttle grip (twist) position: 0 closed, 690-696 fully open, isolated readings up to 730. Percent = raw x 100 / 690, clamped.";
CM_ SG_ 533 TCS_LEVEL_B4 "[confirmed/medium] Copy of the traction-control level (see 0x209 `tcs_level`).";
CM_ SG_ 533 GRIP_CLOSED "[confirmed/medium] Set when the grip is closed or nearly closed: in every frame with grip 0, and also at grip readings up to about 20.";
CM_ SG_ 533 TCS_LEVEL_B5 "[confirmed/medium] Another copy of the traction-control level. B5 = 0x28 + level.";
CM_ SG_ 533 TCS_LEVEL_B6 "[confirmed/medium] Another copy of the traction-control level. B6 = level x 2; bit 7 is set briefly at wake-up.";
CM_ SG_ 533 RIDE_MODE "[confirmed/medium] Copy of the riding mode (see 0x209 `ride_mode`). B7 reads 0x02, 0x42, 0x82 or 0xC2.";
CM_ BO_ 550 "Riding mode and traction-control setting, sent continuously. The value changes only when the rider changes a setting.";
CM_ SG_ 550 RIDE_MODE "[confirmed/medium] Riding mode (see 0x209 `ride_mode`).";
CM_ SG_ 550 TCS_PATTERN "[confirmed/high] The 2-bit traction-control level written twice: 0x5, 0xA or 0xF.";
CM_ SG_ 550 TCS_LEVEL "[confirmed/high] Traction-control setting. Levels 1 and 2 can be switched while riding with the throttle closed; M can be entered or left only at a standstill, and the captures agree: every change into or out of M happened at 0 km/h.";
CM_ SG_ 550 TCS_LEVEL_B5 "[confirmed/high] Copy of `tcs_level`. B5 = 0x20 + level x 4 + `setting_2bit`, which fits every frame of a 42-minute ride.";
CM_ SG_ 550 SETTING_2BIT "[hypothesis/medium] A two-bit rider setting that the ABS unit repeats in 0x268 B4 bits 4..3: the two are equal in every paired frame. It read 1 in every early capture and 2 in every later one, never changing within a session, and it is independent of the TCS level. Candidate: brake control mode (BC1 / BC2). Not verified.";
CM_ BO_ 554 "Two bytes, normally 00 00. One bit follows the rear brake pedal.";
CM_ SG_ 554 BRAKE_SWITCH "[hypothesis/medium] B1 = 0x08 while the rear brake pedal switch (0x268 B4 bit 2) is on: 2209 of 2217 such frames on one ride, 99.2 % on another. Whether the front lever also sets it (which would make it a brake-light signal) is not tested.";
CM_ BO_ 555 "Always `00 00` in every capture.";
CM_ BO_ 558 "Always `00 00` in every capture, at standstill and while riding.";
CM_ BO_ 570 "B0 reads 0x04 for a short time after the bus wakes, then 0x00. B1 and B2 are always 0xFF.";
CM_ SG_ 570 WAKE_PHASE "[hypothesis/low] 0x04 from wake-up for anything between 0.1 s and 54 s, back to 0x00 before the engine is started, and 0x00 from then on whether the engine runs or not (including a 66 s stall). It does not tell a running engine from a stopped one, as we first thought. Use `engine_rpm` for that.";
CM_ BO_ 574 "Coolant temperature, an air temperature and a 16-bit engine-load-like counter, once a second.";
CM_ SG_ 574 COOLANT_TEMP "[confirmed/high] Engine coolant temperature, raw x 0.625 - 30.";
CM_ SG_ 574 AIR_TEMP "[hypothesis/low] An air temperature. raw - 55 gave a believable 13  degC on one cold start, but on another cold start it read 32  degC against 24  degC coolant, and it reads 39-44  degC while riding on a warm day. Either the sensor sits where the engine heats it (intake air) or the scale is wrong. No reference temperature in our captures.";
CM_ SG_ 574 LOAD_COUNTER "[hypothesis/low] 0 with the engine off; rises on a cold start and under hard acceleration (peak 878 on one ride). Probably an injection or torque quantity. Not decoded.";
CM_ BO_ 581 "Always `00` in every capture.";
CM_ BO_ 592 "Three-axis accelerometer. Each axis is a 16-bit word with zero at about 7000 and about 992 counts per g.";
CM_ SG_ 592 ACCEL_X "[confirmed/medium] Longitudinal acceleration, (raw - 7000) / 992. Positive when the bike accelerates. 7000 is the nominal zero (the reset value); this bike reads about 7056 when level and at rest, which includes the mounting angle of the sensor.";
CM_ SG_ 592 ACCEL_Y "[confirmed/medium] Lateral acceleration in the bike frame, (raw - 7000) / 992. The raw value falls when the standing bike is leaned to the left. In a balanced turn the bike leans into the resultant force, so this axis stays small: it moves a few counts per degree of lean in a corner (our estimates run from 2 to 4) against 17.4 counts per degree at standstill.";
CM_ SG_ 592 ACCEL_Z "[confirmed/medium] Vertical acceleration in the bike frame, (raw - 7000) / 992. About +1 g upright and at rest; rises above 1 g in a corner.";
CM_ SG_ 592 POWER_UP_FLAGS "[hypothesis/medium] 0x80 in the first frame, 0x60 for the next 0.4 s, then 0x20 for the rest of the session whether the bike moves or not. An earlier reading of bit 6 as a 'moving' flag was wrong. B7 is always 0x00.";
CM_ BO_ 596 "Three-axis gyroscope. Each axis is a 16-bit word with zero at 30000 and about 100 counts per degree per second. Yaw rate together with wheel speed gives the lean angle in a corner.";
CM_ SG_ 596 ROLL_RATE "[confirmed/medium] Roll rate, (raw - 30000) / 100. Positive when the bike rolls to the right. Peaks when flicking from one side to the other, near zero in a steady corner.";
CM_ SG_ 596 PITCH_RATE "[hypothesis/medium] Pitch rate, (raw - 30000) / 100. Large under braking dive. In a steady corner it shows a steady offset equal to -yaw x tan(lean), which is the turn seen from a leaned sensor and not cross-talk. Hypothesis because nothing outside the IMU was compared with this axis: its scale is inferred from the yaw axis.";
CM_ SG_ 596 YAW_RATE "[confirmed/medium] Yaw rate, (raw - 30000) / 100. Positive in a left turn.";
CM_ BO_ 600 "A second IMU frame with packed fields. One word behaves like a pitch rate with the cornering component removed, which suggests the IMU computes its own attitude; the rest is not decoded.";
CM_ SG_ 600 PITCH_RATE_LEVEL "[hypothesis/medium] Zero at 30000-30004. Follows the 0x254 pitch rate with the -yaw x tan(lean) corner term removed, one frame later (correlation 0.94-0.98 on three rides against 0.52 for the raw pitch rate). Reads as the pitch rate in the road frame.";
CM_ BO_ 604 "Not decoded. B0 takes many values (16 seen) and B1 about a dozen (0x11-0x1C). The low nibbles of B2 and B3 are always 0xB while the high nibbles vary. No correlation with lean, yaw or roll above 0.24.";
CM_ BO_ 608 "Front brake hydraulic pressure as a 16-bit word, sent twice in the frame.";
CM_ SG_ 608 BRAKE_PRESSURE_FRONT "[confirmed/high] Front brake pressure in raw units. 0 with the lever released (small values of 1-6 also occur at rest). Normal riding stays under about 160; hard stops reach 200-840; a full squeeze at standstill reached 1361. The rear pedal does not register here: the word stays 0 in more than 1000 frames where only the rear pedal switch is on. The physical unit is not known.";
CM_ BO_ 616 "A second speed value from the ABS unit, ABS intervention flags and the rear brake pedal switch.";
CM_ SG_ 616 NOT_MOVED_SINCE_START "[hypothesis/medium] 1 from power-on until the speed first passes about 6 km/h, then 0 through every later stop of the ride, and 1 again only in the last 0.2 s before the bus goes quiet. Possibly 'ABS self-check not yet done'. An earlier reading as a low-speed indicator was wrong.";
CM_ SG_ 616 VEHICLE_SPEED "[confirmed/medium] Speed as one 16-bit word, raw / 17.7 = true km/h. It follows the front wheel and lags 0x205 by about 20 ms. 0x205 has 5.7 times the resolution, so prefer it.";
CM_ SG_ 616 ABS_REAR_ACTIVE "[confirmed/high] Rear ABS is intervening. Also set when the rear wheel slows sharply from engine braking on a downshift with no brake applied.";
CM_ SG_ 616 ABS_FRONT_ACTIVE "[confirmed/medium] Front ABS is intervening.";
CM_ SG_ 616 REAR_BRAKE_PEDAL "[confirmed/medium] Rear brake pedal switch.";
CM_ SG_ 616 SETTING_2BIT "[hypothesis/medium] Copy of 0x226 `setting_2bit` (equal in every paired frame). Bit 3 was once read as 'ABS enabled' because it was always 1 in the early captures.";
CM_ BO_ 1025 "0x00 while the bus is alive. 0x80 after the ignition is switched off, for at least half a minute while every other ID has already stopped.";
CM_ SG_ 1025 AFTER_RUN "[hypothesis/medium] 0 for the whole ride, engine running or not. 1 only once the rest of the bus has gone quiet: at least 31 s of this frame alone at the end of one capture (the capture ends first). The sender stays awake for a while after key-off and says so. An earlier reading (0x80 = engine running) was the wrong way round.";
CM_ BO_ 1424 "Always `00 00 00 00` in every capture.";

